## 7. `include/strata/core/conversation_memory.hpp` — Host Memory Telemetry

**Responsibilities:** Provides `conversation_available_memory()` (platform-specific physical RAM reading) and `conversation_mem_available()` (parsing `/proc/meminfo`), plus the admission predicate `conversation_memory_admit()`.

**Proposed change:** Add a `conversation_mem_available_from_file()` that reads from a configurable path (for testing without `/proc`), and add a `cgroup_v2_limit()` function that reads the effective memory limit from cgroup v2 for container-aware admission.

```diff
--- a/include/strata/core/conversation_memory.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/core/conversation_memory.hpp
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #pragma once

 #include <cstdint>
+#include <fstream>
 #include <istream>
 #include <optional>
 #include <string>
@@ -14,4 +15,22 @@
                                       uint64_t allocation, uint64_t floor) {
     return available && *available >= floor && allocation <= *available - floor;
 }

+/// Read MemAvailable from a specific file path (for testing).
+inline std::optional<uint64_t> conversation_mem_available_from_file(const std::string& path) {
+    std::ifstream f(path);
+    if (!f) return std::nullopt;
+    return conversation_mem_available(f);
+}
+
+/// The effective memory limit from cgroup v2 (memory.max), or nullopt if unlimited/unavailable.
+inline std::optional<uint64_t> cgroup_v2_memory_limit() {
+    std::ifstream f("/sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max");
+    if (!f) return std::nullopt;
+    std::string val;
+    if (!(f >> val) || val == "max") return std::nullopt;
+    try { return std::stoull(val); } catch (...) { return std::nullopt; }
+}
+
 } // namespace strata::core
```

**Verification cases:**
1. Write a temp file containing `MemAvailable:   16384 kB\n`; call `conversation_mem_available_from_file()`; confirm it returns `16384 * 1024`.
2. On a Linux host with no cgroup limit, confirm `cgroup_v2_memory_limit()` returns `nullopt`; inside a Docker container with `--memory=4g`, confirm it returns approximately `4294967296`.

